  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1050 AM MST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe upper level trough that brought us rain showers yesterday,\ncontinues to linger south of our area. The low is still close\nenough to give us a fair amount of clouds, especially the further\nsouth and east you go. We could even see some rain showers creep\ninto Hudspeth County later this afternoon, but the rest of the\nregion will stay dry. Today's highs will be warmer than Saturday,\nbut we will still run several degrees below average. By Monday\nafternoon the upper level trough will still be moving very slowly\nas it lumbers into central Texas. That means the clouds will\ncontinue to linger across the region, especially for locations\neast of the Rio Grande. High temperatures on Monday will climb a\nfew degree higher, but we will still have high temperatures a few\ndegrees below average for this time of year. \n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday a developing upper level ridge will be\ngetting itself organized along the west coast. As the ridge \nbuilds to the east we will finally see the clouds banished from \nour region and high temperatures will get up back up to seasonal \naverages or maybe a degree or two above average. Like the trough, \nthe upper level ridge will be slow to move across the area so our \nsunny skies, light winds and high temperatures near average will \ncontinue through Friday. For next weekend, the extended models \ncontinue to show an upper level trough moving into the southwest \nU.S. The EC model is about 12 to 24 hours faster which brings rain\ninto the region on Saturday, while the slower GFS model brings \nthe rain in on Sunday. High temperatures both days will run at or\na little below average.",
